{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792834859,"nonce":"MzqzyOse1WTQRBdp+uFGx6Fy3Jk=","title":"interop: a guest function a Haxe method keeps and calls each step with Buffers","message":"ChimpMonk's script systems (its git-bug 5622c4b) have a guest pass a function to a Haxe method, `scene.system(phase, order, fn:Scene-\u003eFloat-\u003eVoid)`. The engine keeps it and calls it once per fixed step, and the function reads and writes component columns as Buffers the engine hands it.\n\nTested today:\n- a Wren Fn becomes a typed Haxe closure;\n- a Lua function returned to Haxe is called;\n- a Python callable crosses to the core and is called through the bridge.\n\nNot tested: a Python callable or a Lua function kept by a Haxe method, typed as a Haxe function, and called later from Haxe with a Buffer argument whose writes the Haxe side then reads.\n\nWanted: one interop test per language (Wren, Lua, Python), where the guest registers a function with a Haxe class that stores it. Then:\n1. Haxe calls it N times with a Bytes-backed Buffer and a Float.\n2. The guest writes through the buffer each call.\n3. Haxe checks the bytes after each call.\n4. A collection runs between calls, so the kept function and the buffers stay live.","files":null}]}